  "question": {
    "text": "If $y=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1+\\cos 2 \\theta}{1-\\cos 2 \\theta}\\right)}$, then $\\frac{d y}{d \\theta}$ at $\\theta=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}$ is :",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "lZT6s",
      "text": "-2",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "aU3O5",
      "text": "2",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "x4peg",
      "text": "$\\pm 2$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "qaUVn",
      "text": "None of these",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$y=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\cos 2 \\theta}{1-\\cos 2 \\theta}}$<br />$\\Rightarrow y=\\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\cos ^2 \\theta}{2 \\sin ^2 \\theta}}=\\sqrt{\\cot ^2 \\theta}$<br />$\\Rightarrow y=\\cot \\theta$<br />Differentiate w.r.t. ' $\\theta$ ', we get: $\\frac{d y}{d \\theta}=-\\operatorname{cosec}^2 \\theta$<br />Now, $\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{dy}}{\\mathrm{d} \\theta}\\right)_{\\theta=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}}=-\\operatorname{cosec}^2\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)$<br />$=-\\operatorname{cosec}^2\\left(\\pi-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=-\\operatorname{cosec}^2 \\frac{\\pi}{4}=-2$",
    "image": null
  },
